---
title: "Image optimization"
description: "What a local raster image gets for free: a reserved box, a loading policy, and AVIF rungs the build derives from it."
---

Every local raster image on this page is annotated at *render* time with its intrinsic
size, so the reader's layout is reserved before a single byte of the image arrives. The
build then derives AVIF rungs from the same files and offers them through a `<picture>`,
leaving the author's original as the fallback.

## A captioned figure

The first image in a document is the one most likely to be the largest contentful paint,
so it is deliberately **not** lazy: it is fetched eagerly at high priority. Lazy-loading an
above-the-fold image delays the very metric the optimization exists to improve.

![A model fit, at a size that earns two width rungs.](fit-a.png){#fig-opt-wide}

## An inline image

A bare image in a paragraph is not a figure, but it is still a raster file the reader has
to download, so it gets the same treatment minus the numbering: ![a small
fit](fit-small.png) sits in this sentence.

## An image below the smallest rung

`fit-small.png` is 320 pixels wide, narrower than the smallest candidate rung. It gets
exactly one derivative, at its native width.

![The same fit, already smaller than any rung.](fit-small.png){#fig-opt-narrow}

That is not a rounding decision, it is the whole rule: a rung wider than the source would
have to invent pixels, and the codec then has to store them. Re-encoding a 637-pixel-wide
source at an 800-pixel rung was measured at 12,274 bytes against 4,798 at its native width
— larger than the original file it was meant to shrink.

## What is left alone

An SVG has no intrinsic pixel size to state and no raster to transcode, and a `data:` URI
already carries its own pixels, so neither is touched. An image the author has already
shipped as `.webp` or `.avif` is left alone too: that decision has been made.
